\chapter{Parsing, AST shape, and source locations}
\label{ch:72}
\index{Parsing, AST shape, and source locations}

The parser constructs C++ nodes for declarations, statements, expressions, types, and patterns from collected tokens. Precedence and associativity determine which expressions nest. Parentheses can consume parsing depth even when they do not add a durable expression node. The compiler also bounds expression tree depth, since a long sequence of left-associative operators can create a deep tree without deep source parentheses.

\section{Test ambiguous structure before types}
Create a fixture that makes grouping visible in the source AST, then add a parentheses variant with a different tree. Compare both before and after macro expansion to identify which stage changed the structure. Separately test a deep chain that crosses the limit and a large but shallow program. Preserve locations for synthesized nodes when adding a parser desugaring. A subsequent verifier needs the source location for errors such as an out-of-scope loop exit. Parsed AST alone carries no type, loan, or execution evidence.

\section{Count tree levels rather than characters}
Draw the tree for \OnxCodeTerm{8 - 3 - 2}. Its root subtracts two
from the result of the left subtraction; evaluation gives three. For
\OnxCodeTerm{8 - (3 - 2)} the inner subtraction is the right child and
the result is seven. Both trees contain two subtraction nodes and three
integer leaves. A parser test that checks only the number and kinds of
nodes would accept either grouping. Check the child positions or evaluate
with these deliberately unequal results to tell the cases apart.

Now build the first tree again with addition and many more terms. Start
with one literal at depth one. Appending a plus and one literal wraps the
previous tree as the left child of a new root, increasing its depth by
one. The limit fixture places the sum in an expression statement directly
inside the body of \OnxCodeTerm{main}. With 256 ones it checks and runs;
with 257 the compiler reports excessive expression depth. There is no
need to make the input close to the source byte limit to reach it.

Would wrapping that sum in an anonymous function preserve the same inclusive
boundary? The enclosing lambda contributes to the expression tree. The
companion regression accepts 255 terms inside its body but rejects 256 in
that context. Use the original enclosing syntax when minimizing a limit
report: deleting the wrapper may turn the smallest failing tree into a
successful program while leaving every arithmetic operator untouched.
Conversely, many short expression statements can occupy far more source
bytes without extending any one of their left spines.

